          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies differ about delayed prescribing for respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "Delayed prescribing for respiratory infections involves providing instructions for a prescription to be filled only if symptoms fail to improve or worsen by a specified time. This strategy is described as a way to reduce immediate antibiotic use while maintaining patient satisfaction, particularly when a bacterial infection is unlikely but patient expectations or follow-up access make a direct refusal challenging (Infectious diseases society and public health antimicrobial stewardship guidance). Antibiotics are not effective for viral respiratory infections and are reserved for bacterial complications (General clinical reference, \"Influenza\"; General clinical reference, \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\").\n\nHowever, clinical bodies and guidelines differ on how much to encourage this approach. Some authorities recommend delayed prescribing as a tool to reduce immediate antibiotic use. In contrast, others are more cautious, noting that it still frequently results in a prescription being filled and may inadvertently reinforce the belief that antibiotics are necessary for these illnesses (Infectious diseases society and public health antimicrobial stewardship guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
